---
source: crates/sutura-cli/tests/example.rs
expression: pinned.definitions()
---
models:
  customers:
    name: customers
    source: local
    table: dim_customer
    columns:
      - customer_id
      - customer_key
      - region
      - segment
    description: "One row per customer, holding the attributes an answer may be grouped by and nothing\nelse. Nothing here is additive, so a join to it cannot change a measure.\n\n`customer_key` is what every fact carries and `customer_id` is the number a person\nwould quote. No metric groups by the business key: a result with one row per customer\nis a list of customers rather than a measure of anything, and `segment` and `region`\nare the two attributes that put a number in context instead of replacing it."
  daily_usage:
    name: daily_usage
    source: local
    table: fct_usage_daily
    columns:
      - data_gb
      - subscription_key
      - usage_date
      - voice_min
    description: "One row per subscription per day on which it used anything.\n\nIt carries `subscription_key` and no relationship reaches from here to the subscription\nsnapshot, which looks like an omission and is not. The snapshot has one row per\nsubscription per MONTH, so a join on `subscription_key` alone matches every month that\nsubscription existed and multiplies each day of usage by that count. The sum over those\nrows is a wrong number that raises no error anywhere.\n\nThe join that would be correct also constrains the snapshot month to the usage month,\nand a relationship here declares one column on each side. So the relationship is absent\nrather than declared wrongly, and every metric on this model groups by time and by\nnothing else. Usage per product family is a definition this catalog cannot yet express,\nand saying so is better than shipping a number that is quietly several times too large."
  products:
    name: products
    source: local
    table: dim_product
    columns:
      - product_family
      - product_key
      - product_name
    description: "The tariff catalog: one row per sellable product.\n\nThere is no price column, and the absence is the point. A list price is not what a\nsubscription was billed, so a revenue metric that summed it would answer a question\nabout the price list under the name of a question about revenue. The billed figure sits\non the monthly snapshot, where whatever made it differ from the list price has already\nbeen applied."
  subscriptions:
    name: subscriptions
    source: local
    table: fct_subscription_monthly
    columns:
      - churned_in_month
      - contract_term
      - customer_key
      - month
      - mrr_cents
      - product_key
      - status
      - subscription_key
    description: "One row per subscription per month: what that subscription was worth in the month, and\nwhat state it was in at the end of it.\n\nA snapshot, not a log. `month` is the first day of the month being described, so every\nrow of a month carries the same date and a day grain would bucket them all onto the\nfirst. That is why no metric on this model declares one: the answer would be arithmetic\nthat looks like a daily series and is not.\n\nMoney is held in minor units so that a total is exact. A metric that summed a decimal\ncolumn would depend on how two languages happen to print the same bits, and an anchor\ncomparison over that is a test that fails for the wrong reason.\n\n`status` and `churned_in_month` describe the same event from two sides and both are\nhere on purpose. `status` is a state at the end of the month, which is what a revenue\ndefinition narrows on. `churned_in_month` is something that happened inside the month,\nwhich is what a churn count counts. In the general case neither follows from the other:\na subscription that terminates and is restored ends the month in a state that says\nnothing about the termination.\n\n`contract_term` is the one attribute here that belongs to the subscription rather than to the\ncustomer or to the product, so it is the one dimension a metric on this model reaches without a\njoin. Every other dimension in this catalog is declared `via` a relationship, and a catalog\nwhere that was true of all of them would never once compile the plainest case there is: a\ngroup-by key read straight off the fact table, contributing no join of its own. It is\ndeclared on two metrics rather than one so the case survives either of them being rewritten."
relationships:
  subscription_customer:
    name: subscription_customer
    origin_model: subscriptions
    origin_column: customer_key
    target_model: customers
    target_column: customer_key
    join_type: many_to_one
  subscription_product:
    name: subscription_product
    origin_model: subscriptions
    origin_column: product_key
    target_model: products
    target_column: product_key
    join_type: many_to_one
metrics:
  active_subscriptions:
    name: active_subscriptions
    model: subscriptions
    measure:
      simple:
        aggregate: count_distinct
        column: subscription_key
    required_filters:
      - equals:
          column: status
          value: active
    time_column: month
    grains:
      - month
    dimensions:
      product_family:
        name: product_family
        column: product_family
        via: subscription_product
        allowed_values:
          - convergent
          - fixed_internet
          - mobile
          - tv
        description: The kind of product rather than the individual tariff.
      region:
        name: region
        column: region
        via: subscription_customer
        allowed_values:
          - central
          - east
          - north
          - south
          - west
        description: Where the customer is.
      segment:
        name: segment
        column: segment
        via: subscription_customer
        allowed_values:
          - business
          - consumer
          - wholesale
        description: The commercial segment of the customer.
    anchor:
      range:
        start: 2026-06-01
        end: 2026-07-01
      value: "59"
    description: "How many subscriptions were active at the end of the month.\n\nCounts the subscription key distinctly rather than counting rows. Over this catalog the\ntwo agree, because both relationships are many-to-one and neither can fan a row out; they\nwould stop agreeing the moment one could, and the one that keeps meaning \"subscriptions\"\nis the key.\n\nRead it beside `subscription_base`, which is this same count with the status filter taken\naway. The two carry different anchors over the same rows in the same month, and the only\nthing between them is one definitional predicate. That is what `required_filters` buys,\nshown as two numbers rather than asserted in a sentence."
  churn_rate:
    name: churn_rate
    model: subscriptions
    measure:
      ratio:
        numerator:
          count_if: churned_in_month
        denominator:
          aggregate: count_distinct
          column: subscription_key
        zero_denominator: yields_null
    required_filters: []
    time_column: month
    grains:
      - month
    dimensions:
      product_family:
        name: product_family
        column: product_family
        via: subscription_product
        allowed_values:
          - convergent
          - fixed_internet
          - mobile
          - tv
        description: The kind of product rather than the individual tariff.
      region:
        name: region
        column: region
        via: subscription_customer
        allowed_values:
          - central
          - east
          - north
          - south
          - west
        description: Where the customer is.
      segment:
        name: segment
        column: segment
        via: subscription_customer
        allowed_values:
          - business
          - consumer
          - wholesale
        description: The commercial segment of the customer.
    anchor:
      range:
        start: 2026-06-01
        end: 2026-07-01
      value: "0.04838709677419355"
    description: "What share of the month's subscriptions terminated inside it.\n\nThe metric this vocabulary could not express, and the reason it was changed. A rate is a\nconditional count over a distinct count, and while `count_if` was a measure *shape* it\ncould not be a *half* of a ratio: every ingredient was present and there was nowhere to\nwrite it. Read `subscriptions_churned.md` for what that cost, and this frontmatter for what\nit looks like now - the numerator is a term like any other.\n\nThe two halves are the two metrics that used to ship in its place. The numerator is\n`subscriptions_churned`, a conditional count and not `count(churned_in_month)`, which counts\nnon-null rows and would report the whole base as churn. The denominator is\n`subscription_base`, unfiltered, because a base that counted only the survivors would leave\nthe terminated subscriptions in the numerator and out of the denominator at once.\n\nNo status filter, for the reason `subscriptions_churned` gives: churn is an event inside the\nmonth rather than a state at the end of it, and narrowing on the surviving state would remove\nthe rows being counted.\n\n`zero_denominator: yields_null` because a month with no subscriptions at all has no churn\nshare. Zero would say every subscription survived, which is a claim about a month that had\nnone.\n\n## Why this one carries an anchor when the other ratios do not\n\nAn anchor is compared as rendered TEXT, at the metric's coarsest declared grain. That is what\nmakes a float-valued metric hard to anchor, and it is why the other two ratios here carry\nnone: a decimal that had to be converted to binary, or summed in an order nobody specified,\npins how a language prints an expansion rather than pinning a number.\n\nNeither of those reaches this metric, and the reason is arithmetic rather than nerve. Both\nhalves are counts, so both are exact integers in a double whatever order they were summed in,\nand the whole measure is therefore one division of two exactly-represented values - a single\nrounding, with nothing before it that an evaluation order could perturb. `data_per_subscription`\nis the case that is genuinely different: a sum of decimal gigabytes over a count, where two\nengines may legitimately differ in the last place.\n\nBoth halves are also separately certified, which is what makes the anchor readable rather than\nopaque: 3 under `subscriptions_churned` and 62 under `subscription_base`, over this same month.\nA reader checks `0.04838709677419355` against those two, and a run that produced different\ndigits would have done different arithmetic rather than different formatting.\n\nAnd it is the only anchor in this repository that re-executes a conditional count inside a\nratio, which is the shape the vocabulary was changed for. A shape nothing certifies is a shape\nthat merely compiles."
  data_per_subscription:
    name: data_per_subscription
    model: daily_usage
    measure:
      ratio:
        numerator:
          aggregate: sum
          column: data_gb
        denominator:
          aggregate: count_distinct
          column: subscription_key
        zero_denominator: yields_null
    required_filters: []
    time_column: usage_date
    grains:
      - day
      - month
    dimensions: {}
    anchor: ~
    description: "Data volume per subscription, in gigabytes.\n\nA ratio with no definitional filter, which is the other half of what `required_filters`\nis for: this metric means what it measures over every row in range, and there is no\npredicate a reader has to be warned about.\n\nThe denominator counts the subscriptions that appear in the range rather than every\nsubscription that existed during it. A day with no usage is a day with no row here, so\nthis is volume per subscription that used the network, not volume per subscriber. The\nsecond number needs the monthly snapshot, and `daily_usage` says why this catalog cannot\nyet join to it.\n\n`zero_denominator: yields_null` because a range holding no usage rows at all is a range\nwith no figure, not a range whose figure is zero.\n\nNo anchor: a sum of decimal gigabytes divided by a count is a float, and pinning one as\ntext pins how a language prints a binary expansion."
  mean_subscription_mrr:
    name: mean_subscription_mrr
    model: subscriptions
    measure:
      simple:
        aggregate: avg
        column: mrr_cents
    required_filters:
      - equals:
          column: status
          value: active
    time_column: month
    grains:
      - month
    dimensions: {}
    anchor: ~
    description: "What the average active subscription was worth in the month, in minor units.\n\nThe mean of a COLUMN, which is a different definition from every ratio in this catalog and is\ndeclared here to make that difference executable rather than argued. `revenue_per_customer` is\nthe same instinct written as a sum over a distinct count, and its own document says why: a\ncustomer holding three subscriptions is one customer and three rows, so `avg(mrr_cents)` averages\nsubscription-months while that one averages customers. The two answers differ by however much the\nbase fans out. Reading the two files together is the shortest way to see that average revenue is\nnot a definition until somebody has said average over what.\n\nPer SUBSCRIPTION-MONTH, then, and the name says so rather than saying per subscription. A\nsubscription that existed for all six months contributes six rows to a six-month question, and\nthis metric weights it six times. That is the right behaviour for the question it answers - what\na row of this snapshot is typically worth - and the wrong behaviour for a question about\nsubscriptions, which is why the metric that answers that one is a distinct count and not this.\n\n`avg` and nothing else, which is the second reason the document exists. It is the only use of that\naggregate in this repository - the golden suite used to carry an e-commerce catalog of its own, and\nthis directory is what replaced it - and an aggregate no document writes is a generator arm nothing\nrenders and a plan nothing executes.\n\nThe status filter is part of the name, for the reason `recurring_revenue` gives at length: a\nterminated subscription is one the month lost, and averaging its last invoice into what an active\nsubscription is worth answers a different question under a certified name. So `status` is not a\ndimension here either.\n\nCarries no anchor, and this is the plain float case rather than an interesting one. A mean is a\ndivision, a division is not exact in binary, and an anchor is compared as rendered text - so the\ncomparison would pin how a language prints an expansion. The sum and the count it is built from\nare both certified beside it, under `recurring_revenue` and `active_subscriptions`, which is where\na drift in either half would surface first."
  recurring_revenue:
    name: recurring_revenue
    model: subscriptions
    measure:
      simple:
        aggregate: sum
        column: mrr_cents
    required_filters:
      - equals:
          column: status
          value: active
    time_column: month
    grains:
      - month
    dimensions:
      contract_term:
        name: contract_term
        column: contract_term
        via: ~
        allowed_values:
          - annual
          - monthly
        description: "Whether the subscription is on a rolling or a committed term. On the snapshot row itself, so no relationship is named and no join is planned."
      product_family:
        name: product_family
        column: product_family
        via: subscription_product
        allowed_values:
          - convergent
          - fixed_internet
          - mobile
          - tv
        description: The kind of product rather than the individual tariff.
      product_name:
        name: product_name
        column: product_name
        via: subscription_product
        allowed_values: ~
        description: "Declared without a value list, so it can be grouped by and not filtered on."
      region:
        name: region
        column: region
        via: subscription_customer
        allowed_values:
          - central
          - east
          - north
          - south
          - west
        description: Where the customer is.
      segment:
        name: segment
        column: segment
        via: subscription_customer
        allowed_values:
          - business
          - consumer
          - wholesale
        description: "The commercial segment of the customer, reached through one declared relationship."
    anchor:
      range:
        start: 2026-06-01
        end: 2026-07-01
      value: "202121"
    description: "Recurring revenue recognised in the month, in minor units, from active subscriptions\nonly.\n\nThe filter is part of the name. This metric does not mean \"revenue, which you may narrow\nto active if you remember to\": it means the active figure, and a statement that left the\npredicate out would return revenue including terminated subscriptions under a certified\nname. That is the failure arrived at by omission rather than by tampering, so the\npredicate is compiled into every question about this metric and a caller can neither see\nit nor turn it off.\n\n`status` is therefore not a dimension here. A dimension is something a caller may group\nby or filter on, and either would be a way to ask this metric for a number it does not\nmean.\n\n`contract_term` is the one dimension here that names no relationship, and it is declared\nfor what that costs the compiler rather than for the split it reveals. Every other\ndimension on this metric is reached `via` a many-to-one join, so a catalog holding only\nthose would never render the case where a group-by key is read straight off the fact\ntable and contributes no join of its own. It is declared on\n`subscription_months_billed` too, so the case survives either metric being rewritten.\n\nThe anchor is what this metric produced for June 2026 when it was certified. It is\nre-executed on every run and at startup, so a definition that has stopped meaning what it\nclaimed fails readiness rather than answering."
  revenue_per_churned_subscription:
    name: revenue_per_churned_subscription
    model: subscriptions
    measure:
      ratio:
        numerator:
          aggregate: sum
          column: mrr_cents
        denominator:
          count_if: churned_in_month
        zero_denominator: fails
    required_filters: []
    time_column: month
    grains:
      - month
    dimensions: {}
    anchor: ~
    description: "How much recurring revenue the month carried for each subscription it lost, in minor units.\n\nThe mirror of `churn_rate`, and that is the whole reason it is here. There a conditional count\nis the NUMERATOR of a ratio; here it is the DENOMINATOR. Nothing about the vocabulary makes the\ntwo positions different - a term is usable in either half of either shape - but a catalog that\nonly ever wrote one of them would demonstrate half of that claim and read as though it had\ndemonstrated all of it.\n\nNo status filter, and this is the argument `churn_rate` makes about its denominator turned\naround. The rows the denominator counts are the terminated ones, so narrowing the metric to the\nsurviving state would take them out of the numerator while leaving them in the denominator: the\nmonth's revenue would shrink and the count of what it lost would not. The numerator is therefore\nthe whole base, which is also what the name means - revenue the month carried, not revenue it\nkept.\n\n## `fails`, and why it is the only one here that says so\n\n`zero_denominator: fails` says an empty denominator is a fault and not a figure, and it is true of\nthis definition: a month with no terminations does not have a larger revenue-per-termination, it\nhas none, and answering a number for it would answer a different question under this name.\n`churn_rate` and the other two ratios all choose `yields_null`, which is right for a share - a\nmonth with no subscriptions has no churn share - and wrong for this one.\n\n**This is the only document in this repository that chooses the word, and the only one that puts a\nconditional count in a denominator.** The other was the e-commerce catalog the golden suite used to\ncarry, which did both and which this directory replaced, so writing it down here is what stopped the\ncoverage leaving with it. A variant nothing executes looks covered because the enum has a test for\nits spelling, which is exactly how `fails` came to answer the string `inf` under a certified metric\nname; the correction was `Value::Real` refusing a non-finite cell, and what keeps that correction\nhonest is a question that reaches it. It arrived here before that catalog was retired rather than\nbeing noticed missing afterwards.\n\nThe two questions beside it are that pair. June 2026 has three terminations, so it answers a real\nfigure. January 2026 has seventy rows and none of them terminated, which is the shape that reaches\nthe word: the period is not empty, there is a group to answer for, and the denominator is\nnevertheless zero. That question FAILS rather than being refused, and the difference is the\ngovernance one - a refusal is something a caller asked for and may not have, and this caller asked\nsomething the definition permits.\n\n## Why no anchor\n\nNot the float argument the other ratios give, because it would not apply: both halves are exact\nintegers in a double, so this is one division of two exactly-represented values, and `churn_rate`\ncarries an anchor on precisely that reasoning.\n\nThe reason is `fails`. An anchor is one range an author wrote down, re-executed before the bundle\nmay answer anything, so anchoring this metric would mean picking a range where the denominator\nhappens not to be zero and making the readiness of the whole catalog depend on that continuing to\nbe true. The period this metric exists to demonstrate is the one where it has no figure, and an\nanchor cannot be that period. What certifies it instead is the pair of questions above, whose rows\nand whose failure are both pinned."
  revenue_per_customer:
    name: revenue_per_customer
    model: subscriptions
    measure:
      ratio:
        numerator:
          aggregate: sum
          column: mrr_cents
        denominator:
          aggregate: count_distinct
          column: customer_key
        zero_denominator: yields_null
    required_filters:
      - equals:
          column: status
          value: active
    time_column: month
    grains:
      - month
    dimensions:
      segment:
        name: segment
        column: segment
        via: subscription_customer
        allowed_values:
          - business
          - consumer
          - wholesale
        description: The commercial segment of the customer.
    anchor: ~
    description: "Recurring revenue per customer, in minor units, over active subscriptions.\n\nPer CUSTOMER and not per subscription, which is the whole reason this is a ratio rather\nthan an average. `avg(mrr_cents)` is the mean of a column: it averages whatever rows the\nstatement produced, and those rows are subscription-months. A customer holding three\nsubscriptions is one customer and three rows, so the two answers differ by however much\nthe base fans out, and only one of them is the number people mean by revenue per\ncustomer.\n\n`zero_denominator` is written out because a month with no active subscriptions has to mean\nsomething and both answers are defensible. Here it means null: a month with no customers\nis a month with no revenue per customer, which is a different statement from a month\nwhose figure is zero.\n\nCarries no anchor. A division is not exact in binary, so pinning one as text would pin a\nformatting decision rather than a number, and the sum it is built from is already\nanchored under `recurring_revenue`, which is where a drift would show up first."
  subscription_base:
    name: subscription_base
    model: subscriptions
    measure:
      simple:
        aggregate: count_distinct
        column: subscription_key
    required_filters: []
    time_column: month
    grains:
      - month
    dimensions:
      product_family:
        name: product_family
        column: product_family
        via: subscription_product
        allowed_values:
          - convergent
          - fixed_internet
          - mobile
          - tv
        description: The kind of product rather than the individual tariff.
      region:
        name: region
        column: region
        via: subscription_customer
        allowed_values:
          - central
          - east
          - north
          - south
          - west
        description: Where the customer is.
      segment:
        name: segment
        column: segment
        via: subscription_customer
        allowed_values:
          - business
          - consumer
          - wholesale
        description: The commercial segment of the customer.
    anchor:
      range:
        start: 2026-06-01
        end: 2026-07-01
      value: "62"
    description: "How many subscriptions the month held, whatever state they ended it in.\n\nUnfiltered on purpose, and that is the definition rather than an oversight: this is the\ndenominator a churn share is taken against, and a base that counted only the survivors\nwould leave the terminated subscriptions in the numerator and out of the denominator at\nthe same time.\n\nIt is `active_subscriptions` with one predicate removed. The two carry separate anchors\nfor exactly that reason. If a later edit dropped the predicate from the other one, the\ntwo definitions would silently become the same definition, and the anchor is what says\nso."
  subscription_months_billed:
    name: subscription_months_billed
    model: subscriptions
    measure:
      simple:
        aggregate: count
        column: subscription_key
    required_filters: []
    time_column: month
    grains:
      - month
    dimensions:
      contract_term:
        name: contract_term
        column: contract_term
        via: ~
        allowed_values:
          - annual
          - monthly
        description: "Whether the subscription is on a rolling or a committed term. On the snapshot row itself, so no relationship is named and no join is planned."
      product_family:
        name: product_family
        column: product_family
        via: subscription_product
        allowed_values:
          - convergent
          - fixed_internet
          - mobile
          - tv
        description: The kind of product rather than the individual tariff.
      region:
        name: region
        column: region
        via: subscription_customer
        allowed_values:
          - central
          - east
          - north
          - south
          - west
        description: Where the customer is.
      segment:
        name: segment
        column: segment
        via: subscription_customer
        allowed_values:
          - business
          - consumer
          - wholesale
        description: The commercial segment of the customer.
    anchor:
      range:
        start: 2026-06-01
        end: 2026-07-01
      value: "62"
    description: "How many subscription-months the period billed.\n\nA count of ROWS and not of subscriptions, which is what the name says and what the aggregate\ndoes. `subscription_base` is the same column under `count_distinct`, and the two are the same\nnumber over this catalog for a reason worth stating rather than relying on: the snapshot holds one\nrow per subscription per month, this metric declares only the month grain, so inside any one\nbucket a subscription key cannot appear twice. They would stop agreeing the moment either half of\nthat changed - a quarter grain, or a model that split a month into two rows - and only one of them\nwould still mean what its name says.\n\nSo the pair is the same contrast `active_subscriptions` and `subscription_base` draw with a\npredicate, drawn here with an aggregate instead: two documents, one differing word, two anchors\nover the same month. If a later edit swapped this one to `count_distinct` the two definitions\nwould silently become the same definition, and there would be nothing but the name to say so.\n\n**It is also the only plain `count` in this repository**, now that the e-commerce catalog the golden\nsuite used to carry has been retired in favour of this one. Everything else counting\nthings here counts them distinctly, or counts a condition; a `count` that no document writes is a\ngenerator arm nothing renders. `subscriptions_churned` says the other half of that: a count of a\nBOOLEAN column counts the false rows too, so `count` is right over a key and wrong over a flag,\nand the two documents are the two sides of the same mistake.\n\n`contract_term` is declared without `via`, which makes this one of the two metrics here that\nreaches a group-by key that contributes no join - the plainest key the compiler resolves, and the\none a catalog of exclusively joined dimensions would never once compile. `recurring_revenue`\ndeclares it too, so the case survives either metric being rewritten.\n\nNo definitional filter, on purpose. A subscription-month was billed whatever state the\nsubscription ended the month in, and a terminated one was billed for the month it terminated in -\nwhich is the same reason `subscription_base` carries no filter, and the reason both of them can\nserve as a denominator."
  subscriptions_churned:
    name: subscriptions_churned
    model: subscriptions
    measure:
      simple:
        count_if: churned_in_month
    required_filters: []
    time_column: month
    grains:
      - month
    dimensions:
      product_family:
        name: product_family
        column: product_family
        via: subscription_product
        allowed_values:
          - convergent
          - fixed_internet
          - mobile
          - tv
        description: The kind of product rather than the individual tariff.
      region:
        name: region
        column: region
        via: subscription_customer
        allowed_values:
          - central
          - east
          - north
          - south
          - west
        description: Where the customer is.
      segment:
        name: segment
        column: segment
        via: subscription_customer
        allowed_values:
          - business
          - consumer
          - wholesale
        description: The commercial segment of the customer.
    anchor:
      range:
        start: 2026-06-01
        end: 2026-07-01
      value: "3"
    description: "How many subscriptions terminated inside the month.\n\n`count_if` rather than a count of the column, and the difference is a wrong number that\nraises no error: `count(churned_in_month)` counts the rows where the column is not null,\nwhich is every row, so it would report the whole base as churn. How the condition is\nspelled differs between data systems, which is the problem of the generator and exactly\nthe kind of thing that should not be in a catalog document.\n\nNo status filter, because churn is an event inside the month rather than a state at the\nend of it. Narrowing on the surviving state would remove the rows being counted.\n\nThis is the numerator of a rate and not the rate itself, which is a decision about the\nbusiness rather than a limit of the vocabulary. A count of terminations is the figure\nsomebody reconciles against a churn report; the share is `churn_rate`, declared beside it\nover the same numerator and `subscription_base`'s denominator. Both are certified, and the\none that answers \"how many\" is not the one that answers \"what fraction\".\n\nIt used to be a limit of the vocabulary, and the note that said so is worth keeping as a\nrecord of what changed. `count_if` was a measure *shape*, a sibling of `ratio` rather than\na term inside one, so a rate over a conditional count had every ingredient present and\nnowhere to write it. The fix was to make the extensible axis the term instead of the shape:\n`simple` and `ratio` are the two shapes, `aggregate` and `count_if` are the two terms, and\neither term is usable in either half of either shape."
  voice_minutes:
    name: voice_minutes
    model: daily_usage
    measure:
      simple:
        aggregate: sum
        column: voice_min
    required_filters: []
    time_column: usage_date
    grains:
      - day
      - month
    dimensions: {}
    anchor: ~
    description: "Outgoing voice minutes.\n\nHere so the plainest shape in the vocabulary appears in the example unadorned: one\naggregate over one column, no definitional filter, no join, no ratio. Most certified\nmetrics look like this, and a catalog whose every entry needed a paragraph of\njustification would be a catalog nobody trusted.\n\nSame grains as `data_per_subscription`, and the same reason for carrying no anchor: a sum\nof decimals is a float, so an anchor written as text would pin a formatting decision\nrather than a number."
